  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS000630",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All listed candidates are direct matches to the target trait (thyroid carcinoma / thyroid cancer by trait_efo and phenotyping). Several models report only full-model AUROC (often covariate-influenced) and many come from framework-style or repository/benchmark contexts, which the provided domain knowledge cautions against over-weighting unless PRS-only metrics are clearly superior. PGS000630 (Cancer PRSweb; GWAS Hits; 10 variants) has a coherent thyroid-cancer endpoint, uses only basic covariates (age, sex, batch, PCs 1–4), and shows comparatively strong effect-size evidence (OR per SD 1.598, 95% CI 1.439–1.775; top 1% vs rest OR 3.53) alongside a moderate full-model AUROC (0.626). Competing disease-focused single-study candidates include PGS000208 (full-model AUROC 0.751) but its covariates include family history (a heavier, less PRS-comparable covariate), and PGS001354 has very high AUROC (0.83) but is a restricted endpoint (subsequent thyroid cancer in childhood cancer survivors), making it less aligned to generic thyroid carcinoma risk. Newer multi-ancestry preprint models (PGS005259/260) have promising AUROC/OR but have unknown covariates and limited visible evaluation details here. Because the evidence base relies largely on non-PRS-only metrics and heterogeneous study archetypes/endpoints, the selection is direct-match but not high-certainty."
}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS001267","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All 9 candidates are direct matches to ankylosing spondylitis by trait_efo, and most have endpoint-faithful phenotyping_reported ("Ankylosing spondylitis"). Two UKB framework/portability families (PGS001267/PGS001268 from the pan-trait sparse UKB ‘813 traits’ paper; PGS001876/PGS002089 from the UKB portability ‘245 polygenic scores’ paper) provide PRS-comparable metrics (PGS-only AUROC/R2 for PGS001267/PGS001268; partial-r for PGS001876/PGS002089). The East Asian Taiwanese study family (PGS003420–PGS003424) reports only full-model AUROC with unknown covariates, which domain guidance says is not a valid substitute for PRS-only performance and is hard to compare across models.

Between the two candidates with explicit PRS-only metrics, PGS001268 has slightly higher reported AUROC/R2 but uses a time-to-event phenotype (TTE ankylosing spondylitis), which is a stated endpoint warning signal when the target is generic disease risk. PGS001267 uses a straightforward disease endpoint and reports PRS-only AUROC=0.7265 and PRS-only R2=0.0988 (and full-model AUROC with basic covariates), providing the cleanest directly comparable performance evidence without the time-to-event endpoint penalty.

Main limitation: PGS001267 (and PGS001268) come from a pan-trait sparse UKB framework (explicitly flagged in the domain knowledge as tending to generalize poorly), and there is no clearly disease-focused multi-cohort AS PRS in this candidate set to resolve that concern; other alternatives either lack PRS-only metrics (Taiwanese family) or provide only partial-r without AUC/R2 (portability family)."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS005262","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Direct-match candidates are present for the target concept (nodular goiter), including (i) nontoxic multinodular goiter scores (PGS001814, PGS002022) and (ii) benign nodular goiter scores (PGS005261–PGS005263). Two additional models (PGS005273, PGS005274) are case-control contrasts of thyroid carcinoma vs benign nodular goiter and are less directly aligned to generic nodular goiter risk because the endpoint is a differential diagnosis contrast rather than goiter status alone.

Among the goiter-status models, PGS005262 is preferred because it is phenotype-aligned (benign nodular goiter) and comes from a disease-focused multi-cohort study family, and it has the strongest visible discriminative signal within that aligned sibling set (highest reported AUROC among the benign nodular goiter models: 0.5933; and the largest per-SD effect size evidence shown: OR 1.408). The UKB portability-framework models (PGS001814, PGS002022) are also endpoint-aligned but come from a portability sweep and only report weak partial correlations (~0.024–0.028), which is limited evidence for real-world PRS performance.

Limitation: for the preferred model family (PGS005261–PGS005263), covariates are listed as Unknown and the AUROC shown is a full-model AUROC (not explicitly PGS-only), reducing comparability and preventing a high-confidence pick; additionally, the target trait wording (nodular goiter) is broader than the specific benign/nontoxic labels, so exact endpoint equivalence cannot be fully verified from the provided metadata alone."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS005262","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All 7 candidates are direct or near-direct matches to the target concept (nodular goiter), with two targeting nontoxic multinodular goiter (PGS001814, PGS002022), three targeting benign nodular goiter (PGS005261–PGS005263), and two targeting a case-control contrast that includes benign nodular goiter (PGS005273–PGS005274). For a generic “nodular goiter” target, the benign nodular goiter models (PGS005261–PGS005263) have the closest endpoint alignment by trait_reported/trait_efo, while the portability framework UKB models (PGS001814/PGS002022) are from a known pan-trait portability study and have only weak partial-correlation evidence (~0.024–0.0277) and no PRS-only AUC/R2.

Within the benign nodular goiter cluster, PGS005262 is best supported by the visible performance metadata: it has the highest reported AUROC among the benign-goiter models (0.5933 vs 0.5876 and 0.5854) and the strongest effect size signal (OR per SD 1.408 vs 1.362 and 1.048), and it uses a genome-wide continuous shrinkage method (PRSCS) with a large variant count (~1.09M), which is structurally favorable when endpoints are comparable.

The main limitation is that for PGS005262 the AUROC shown is a full-model AUROC (not explicitly PGS-only), and covariates are listed as Unknown, reducing comparability and preventing a high-confidence choice. In addition, multiple sibling models from the same preprint family are close, and there is no explicit PGS-only metric to cleanly separate them."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS000054","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All five candidates are direct matches on concept (`trait_efo` = late-onset Alzheimer's disease). Endpoint fidelity is best for PGS000053/PGS000054/PGS000334/PGS004918, which explicitly phenotype late-onset AD (PGS000053/54 are familial LOAD; PGS000334 and PGS004918 are late-onset AD). PGS002289 is weaker on endpoint fidelity because its `phenotyping_reported` is a cognitive test age-interaction outcome rather than AD diagnosis.

Among the endpoint-faithful set, only PGS000053 and PGS000054 report PRS-relevant effect sizes (OR per SD). Per the provided domain guidance, OR magnitude is a strong discriminator when PGS-only AUC/R² are missing (as here). PGS000054 shows stronger association (OR=1.73, 95% CI 1.57–1.93) than PGS000053 (OR=1.29, 95% CI 1.21–1.37), supporting better discriminative signal.

Key limitation: evidence is incomplete/partly non-comparable across models—no PGS-only AUROC/R² is provided for any candidate; PGS000334 reports a high R² (0.191) but it is not explicitly PRS-only and is flagged by the domain knowledge as potentially non-comparable/suspect for simple C+T pipelines with unknown covariates. Also, PGS000054 was evaluated in AMR (Hispanic/Latin American) rather than EUR, so without a specified intended deployment ancestry this reduces certainty. Therefore the best choice is suggested but not high-confidence."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY","best_model_id":"PGS005217","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All four candidates are direct matches within the peripheral vascular disease spectrum: two are labeled "Peripheral vascular disease, unspecified" (PGS001843, PGS002055; trait_efo=peripheral vascular disease) and two are "Peripheral artery disease" (PGS005158, PGS005217; trait_efo=peripheral arterial disease). Given the target is a broad umbrella (peripheral vascular disease), PAD is a close, clinically central manifestation and remains a valid direct match.

PGS005217 is best supported because it comes from a disease-focused PAD study ("Polygenic Prediction of Peripheral Artery Disease and Major Adverse Limb Events.") with a clear incident PAD endpoint and basic covariates (age, sex, PCs), and it reports a strong PRS-associated effect size (HR per SD 1.66, 95% CI 1.61–1.71) alongside a reported C-index (0.731). In contrast, the two UKB portability-framework PVD-"unspecified" scores (PGS001843/PGS002055) have only very small partial correlations (~0.015–0.018) and are from a known portability framework context, which the provided domain guidance cautions often underperforms disease-focused alternatives. PGS005158 is also PAD but is a 19-variant GWAS-hits score in a restricted phenotype context (PAD in type 2 diabetes) with heavy clinical covariate adjustment and only a modest HR (1.13), making it less suitable as a general direct-match PRS for the broad target.

Main limitation: the selected model targets PAD (not the broader/unspecified PVD label) and reports full-model discrimination (C-index) rather than a clearly PRS-only AUC/R2, so confidence is not high despite strong HR evidence and clean covariates."}

